# The planner (23.10), a program of the chain (languages 1-3): the pass over the text of language 2 that `l3c` writes in pack mode (the
# first word of the text of language 3 is `pack`). stdin: that text and its zero byte; stdout: the same text with the blocks packed
# into fewer layers, and a zero byte (what `l2l` reads, twice). The chain of the build with the planner is
#     text of language 3 (pack ...) -> l3c -> l3p -> l2l -> seed -> the image (P = 3)
# The reference in Python is tower/l3ctext.py (pack_pass), which also tells what is done and why; this text does the same on the
# chain and a test compares the bytes. A line is read whole (getline: the characters in lb, the first four blank-separated tokens: their
# values, text and kind); the lines outside a block go out as they are (the positions po of the text and pn of the new text
# move together), a marker `# a <k> <pad>` is a pad to recount, a block (the label :b...) is read to the jump which leaves it, planned
# (rows to layers: the list scheduler of l3.py without direct writes, over the window of 8 rows before) and written. The rows of the
# block are the units 0..nu-1 (a row of the text and its jump), the rows which do something are the ops 0..nx-1; the tables hold
# for each op the bits it reads and writes (rlo rhi wlo whi) as intervals of numbers. Errors (stderr, halt; the lines of the blocks before the one in hand are out): `l3p: bad text`
# (a line that is not of the format, the zero byte inside a block or a pad, a block that reads the text of its own rows), `l3p: too big` (a block of more than 254 layers).
# The tables: cls (a byte: zero, blank, line break, &), hexv (a hex digit and 16), fcl (the first byte of a line), tbase (the place of a table
# of the library above the memory), mu (80 bits for a slot), mask, p2e, inc8 as in l3c.tw. Their numbers are made by tower/l3ctext.py (p_tables_text); a test compares them.

